{
  "thesis": "A stale bus ticket can be treated not as a dead artifact but as recyclable material: its failed plan can be selectively broken down so that its surviving details generate a new possibility.",
  "mechanism": "Like ubiquitin-marked cellular debris, the ticket's obsolete itinerary is tagged for dismantling; irrelevant parts are discarded while usable fragments—date, route, fare—are returned to the imagination as fuel for a new journey.",
  "application": "Use tomorrow's date to make the ticket a live prompt, digest the abandoned journey, and build the next scene from what survives. \"The ticket was yellowed at the edges, but the ink was sharp: Route 17, departing at 6:40, tomorrow. It had been bought thirty years ago for a journey someone else had abandoned, and the envelope carried no return address. I looked up the route and found it had been dismantled years ago, most of its stops folded into other lines. So I did what the dead timetable no longer could: I marked what still worked. The fare. The first terminus. A seat on the first bus out. At dawn I would go there, carrying the old ticket as the only surviving part of the plan, and let tomorrow decide whether it could become the beginning of another.\"",
  "first_step": "Write one line listing the ticket's obsolete details and circle the single usable fragment that can seed the next scene.",
  "source_ids": [
    "collider_selective_autophagy"
  ]
}

